<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Dragon Aspect's Ancient Dragonborn summon.

    NOT IN SPEC.md - specified by the user directly. He is a RESCUE: casting the three-word
    shout at full health summons nobody. He arrives when the Dovahkiin is already hurt at cast
    time, or is downed while the shout is running.

    THE HEDIFF IS THE SAFETY MECHANISM, exactly as Dovahkiin_DeadPuppet is for Soul Tear.
    RISKS.md section 9 names temporary pawns as the top save-corruption risk in this mod, and
    the answer there was to make the pawn ALWAYS DOOMED: incurable, untendable, non-removable,
    and it ends the pawn when it expires. Every exit path therefore already terminates, and
    save -> load carries nothing bespoke.

      isBad             false  - it is not an affliction, it is what he IS
      tendable          false  - nothing can extend his stay
      makesSickThought  false  - no colonist reacts to him being temporary
      scenarioCanAdd    false  - never grantable from a scenario

    Do NOT add HediffComp_Disappears here. The lifetime is counted by Hediff_AncientDragonborn
    itself, because expiry must VANISH THE PAWN rather than merely remove a hediff - and a comp
    that removed it would strand a permanent summoned colonist, which is the exact failure the
    whole design exists to prevent.
  -->
  <HediffDef>
    <defName>Dovahkiin_AncientDragonborn</defName>
    <label>ancient dragonborn</label>
    <description>This warrior is a shard of the Dovahkiin's own soul, held in the world by the Thu'um alone. When the shout fades, so does he.</description>
    <hediffClass>Dovahkiin.Hediff_AncientDragonborn</hediffClass>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <tendable>false</tendable>
    <makesSickThought>false</makesSickThought>
    <scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
    <maxSeverity>1.0</maxSeverity>
    <defaultLabelColor>(0.42, 0.62, 0.95)</defaultLabelColor>
    <comps>
      <!--
        Vanilla's own invisibility, the same one Become Ethereal uses, so the pawn underneath
        fades and the spectral Dragon Aspect armour is what the player sees.

        DECLARED EXACTLY AS ROYALTY'S PsychicInvisibility DOES: a plain HediffCompProperties
        carrying compClass. There is NO HediffCompProperties_Invisibility type in 1.4 - the
        first version of this def invented one, along with a visibleToPlayer field. Both would
        have been logged as XML errors and silently dropped, leaving him fully visible while
        every comment here claimed otherwise. Checked against the real def, not from memory.

        The Royalty *def* is DLC, but the CLASS is in Verse inside Assembly-CSharp, so this is
        baseline-safe - same reasoning that made PawnJumper usable.

        KNOWN SIDE EFFECT, FLAGGED FOR PLAYTEST: vanilla invisibility also makes a pawn hard
        to TARGET. On Become Ethereal that is arguably faithful. On a rescue summon it means
        enemies largely ignore him, so he is far harder to kill than his health suggests -
        which is a balance question, not a bug. TESTS/phase2j.md asks about it. If it is
        unwanted, delete this one comp and he becomes an ordinary visible ally.
      -->
      <li Class="HediffCompProperties">
        <compClass>HediffComp_Invisibility</compClass>
      </li>
    </comps>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <statOffsets>
          <MoveSpeed>0.4</MoveSpeed>
          <!--
            Physical armour. Started at 1.00 at the user's request, then cut 25% to 0.75 on
            their instruction once the scale was spelled out.

            RimWorld armour is a FRACTION, so 0.75 is 75 percentage points and he had none
            before. Even here conventional weapons are heavily blunted, and vanilla invisibility
            already makes him hard to target, so he stays very durable. Intended: he is a ghost,
            time-boxed to 1.5 in-game hours once a day, only while the Dovahkiin is in trouble.

            THE LADDER, so these keep their order if either is ever retuned:
              Ancient Dragonborn   0.75 sharp / 0.75 blunt   <- this
              Call of Valor hero   0.66 sharp / 0.33 blunt   <- a real steel plate suit
            The hero is deliberately the weaker of the two, per the user, and his numbers are
            not invented: vanilla plate armour carries StuffEffectMultiplierArmor 0.73 and steel
            has StuffPower_Armor_Sharp 0.9 / Blunt 0.45, giving 0.657 and 0.329. Rounded. That
            also means the hero is much softer against BLUNT, which is what plate really is.

            NOT heat or frost: physical only. His element comes from the breath he rolls.
          -->
          <ArmorRating_Sharp>0.75</ArmorRating_Sharp>
          <ArmorRating_Blunt>0.75</ArmorRating_Blunt>
        </statOffsets>
        <statFactors>
          <IncomingDamageFactor>0.75</IncomingDamageFactor>
        </statFactors>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </HediffDef>

  <!--
    CALL OF VALOR'S HERO OF SOVNGARDE.

    THE SAME DOOMED CONSTRUCTION as the Ancient Dragonborn above, and for the same reason:
    RISKS.md section 9 names temporary pawns as the top save-corruption risk in this mod, and
    the only thing that made one safe was making it always doomed. Incurable, untendable,
    non-removable, and it ends the pawn when it expires. Do NOT add HediffComp_Disappears -
    expiry must VANISH THE PAWN, not merely remove a hediff, or a permanent summoned colonist
    is left standing.

    hediffClass is Hediff_CallOfValor, a SUBCLASS of Hediff_AncientDragonborn that overrides one
    thing: he knows Unrelenting Force and Frost Breath, and no fire. Everything else - the
    doomed timer, the ally-safety cone check, joining the Dovahkiin's fights, dropping the
    player faction before destruction so Ideology does not throw - is inherited and playtested.

    HE IS DELIBERATELY WEAKER THAN THE ANCIENT DRAGONBORN, per the user, and the numbers are
    not invented. Vanilla plate armour carries StuffEffectMultiplierArmor 0.73 and steel has
    StuffPower_Armor_Sharp 0.9 / Blunt 0.45, giving 0.657 and 0.329 - rounded to 0.66 and 0.33.
    That also means he is much softer against BLUNT, which is what plate really is:

      Ancient Dragonborn   0.75 sharp / 0.75 blunt
      Call of Valor        0.66 sharp / 0.33 blunt   <- this, a real steel plate suit

    He does get TWICE the lifetime - 30000 ticks against 15000 - which is where his value is.
  -->
  <HediffDef>
    <defName>Dovahkiin_CallOfValor</defName>
    <label>hero of sovngarde</label>
    <description>A champion of Sovngarde, called back across the void by the Thu'um. He answers, he fights, and when the echo fades he returns to the hall of heroes.</description>
    <hediffClass>Dovahkiin.Hediff_CallOfValor</hediffClass>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <tendable>false</tendable>
    <makesSickThought>false</makesSickThought>
    <scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
    <maxSeverity>1.0</maxSeverity>
    <defaultLabelColor>(0.72, 0.86, 1.0)</defaultLabelColor>
    <comps>
      <!-- Vanilla's own invisibility, as above. A plain HediffCompProperties carrying
           compClass, exactly as Royalty's PsychicInvisibility declares it: there is NO
           HediffCompProperties_Invisibility type in 1.4, and inventing one is an XML error
           that gets silently dropped, leaving a visible pawn under comments claiming
           otherwise. The class lives in Verse, so this is baseline-safe.

           Same flagged side effect: vanilla invisibility also makes a pawn hard to TARGET,
           so he is harder to kill than 0.66/0.33 suggests. Delete this one comp and he
           becomes an ordinary visible ally. -->
      <li Class="HediffCompProperties">
        <compClass>HediffComp_Invisibility</compClass>
      </li>
    </comps>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <statOffsets>
          <ArmorRating_Sharp>0.66</ArmorRating_Sharp>
          <ArmorRating_Blunt>0.33</ArmorRating_Blunt>
        </statOffsets>
        <statFactors>
          <!-- Deliberately NOT the Ancient Dragonborn's 0.75. He is the weaker of the two by
               design; his advantage is that he stays twice as long. -->
          <IncomingDamageFactor>0.85</IncomingDamageFactor>
        </statFactors>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </HediffDef>

</Defs>
